/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Science: how the world really works */
WB.L("magnetism", "science", "beginner", "Magnetism: the invisible pull",
  "A fridge magnet, a compass needle and the southern lights are all showing you the same hidden force at work.",
  [
    ["A rock that points north", "People used magnetic rocks and compasses for thousands of years before anyone could explain why they work.",
      "Long before anyone understood it, people noticed that certain dark rocks pulled iron towards them. These lodestones are made of magnetite that has become naturally magnetised, possibly by lightning strikes. In China, lodestone spoons were said to swing round to point in a fixed direction, and by around the eleventh century Chinese sailors steered with magnetised needles. The compass later spread to the Arab world and Europe, where it helped make the great ocean voyages possible.\n\nFor centuries, nobody could say why a sliver of iron should care which way north was. The answer lay deep inside atoms, and deep inside the Earth itself."],
    ["Tiny magnets lining up", "Every electron acts like a tiny magnet, and a material is magnetic when enough of them line up.",
      "Each electron acts like a tiny bar magnet, thanks to a property called spin. In most materials, including wood, plastic, water and your body, electrons pair up and their effects cancel. In a few, unpaired electrons add together, and when enough point the same way, the object is magnetic.\n\nIron, nickel and cobalt are the classic magnetic metals. Their atoms group into domains, each already magnetised. In a nail the domains point every which way and cancel. Bring a strong magnet near and they swing into line, so the nail becomes a magnet for a while. Permanent magnets keep their domains aligned. Aluminium is barely magnetic, so a magnet ignores a soft drink can but grips a steel tin.\n\nHeat scrambles the order. Above the Curie point, about 770 degrees Celsius for iron, the magnetism disappears."],
    ["One force, many uses", "Moving electric charges make magnetism and moving magnets make electricity, which is why generators, motors and scanners work.",
      "In 1820 Hans Christian Oersted saw a compass needle twitch when current flowed in a nearby wire: electricity can create magnetism. In 1831 Michael Faraday showed the reverse. Moving a magnet near a coil makes a current flow: electromagnetic induction. In the 1860s James Clerk Maxwell showed electricity and magnetism are one force, and predicted waves travelling at the speed of light. Light, radio waves and X-rays are all electromagnetic waves.\n\nFaraday's discovery powers your life. Nearly every power station spins a magnet and a coil past each other so induction pushes current through the coil. Motors run the trick backwards, in your fridge, washing machine and car windows. Speakers move a cone with a magnet and coil. MRI scanners line up hydrogen atoms in your body, then listen to their faint radio signals."],
    ["Earth's magnetic shield", "Swirling molten iron deep underground makes Earth a giant magnet that deflects the solar wind.",
      "The Earth behaves like a huge magnet, which is why compasses work. Its field is generated about 3,000 kilometres below your feet, in the outer core, where molten iron churns and flows like a natural dynamo. The field reaches far into space and deflects most of the solar wind, a stream of charged particles from the Sun.\n\nSome particles are funnelled towards the poles, where they hit gases in the upper atmosphere and make them glow. In the Southern Hemisphere that glow is the aurora australis, sometimes seen from Tasmania.\n\nThe field is restless. The magnetic poles wander, and every so often the whole field flips. The last full reversal happened roughly 780,000 years ago."],
    ["Animals with a compass", "Many animals sense Earth's magnetic field to navigate, although exactly how is still being worked out.",
      "Humans need a compass, but many animals seem to carry one inside them. Experiments show that migratory birds, sea turtles, salmon and even some insects use Earth's magnetic field to find their way. Young loggerhead turtles appear to read the field's strength and angle to stay on course across thousands of kilometres of ocean.\n\nHow they sense it remains one of biology's lingering puzzles. One leading idea is that light-sensitive proteins in birds' eyes react to magnetism, perhaps letting them 'see' the field as a faint pattern over their vision. Another is that tiny grains of magnetite in some animals' bodies act like compass needles. Both may be true in different species."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What are naturally magnetic rocks called?", ["Moonstones","Lodestones","Soapstones","Bloodstones"], 1, "Lodestones are naturally magnetised magnetite, and they led to the compass."],
    [1, "Why is a nail not normally a magnet, even though iron is magnetic?", ["Iron atoms have no electrons to line up","Heat has permanently destroyed its magnetism","Rust has permanently destroyed its magnetism","Its domains point every which way and cancel"], 3, "Magnetism appears only when enough tiny magnets line up in the same direction."],
    [2, "What happens when you move a magnet near a coil of wire?", ["Nothing, unless the wire is hot","A current flows through the wire","The wire becomes a permanent magnet","The magnet loses its strength"], 1, "This is electromagnetic induction, the principle behind nearly every power station."],
    [3, "What causes the aurora australis?", ["Sunlight bouncing off polar ice sheets","Lightning flashing high above the clouds","Charged solar particles hitting polar gases","Moonlight scattering through ice crystals"], 2, "Earth's magnetic field steers the solar wind towards the poles, where it lights up the sky."],
    [4, "Which statement about animals and magnetism is accurate?", ["Only birds have ever been shown to sense it","The sense is known to work only at night","Researchers disproved the idea in the 1990s","The exact mechanism is still being studied"], 3, "Many animals use the field to navigate, but the sensing mechanism is still debated."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A rock that points north, and a force that links light to your fridge door."});

WB.L("sound", "science", "beginner", "How sound travels",
  "Every voice, song and thunderclap is just air being squeezed and stretched, and the physics explains everything from echoes to sonic booms.",
  [
    ["A wave you can't see", "Sound is a travelling ripple of pressure, not a stream of air rushing from the source to your ear.",
      "Clap your hands and you shove the air between them outwards. Those molecules bump their neighbours, which bump theirs, and a ripple of squashed and stretched air spreads in every direction. That ripple is sound. It is a pressure wave, and the air itself barely moves: each molecule nudges back and forth and passes the push along, like a ripple running down a slinky.\n\nWhen the wave reaches you, it pushes and pulls on your eardrum, which vibrates in step. Tiny bones in your middle ear pass the vibration to the fluid-filled cochlea, where thousands of hair cells turn it into nerve signals. Your brain turns those signals into a friend's laugh or a favourite song."],
    ["The speed of sound", "Sound travels about 343 metres per second in air, and much faster through water and solids.",
      "In air at about 20 degrees Celsius, sound travels about 343 metres every second, roughly a kilometre in three seconds. Light is nearly a million times faster, which is why you see lightning before you hear thunder. Count the seconds between flash and rumble, then divide by three to estimate the storm's distance in kilometres.\n\nSound is faster in warm air than cold, and much faster through liquids and solids, where closely packed particles pass the push along. In seawater it travels at roughly 1,500 metres per second, and in steel several times faster again. That is why you can hear an approaching train through the rails before you hear it through the air."],
    ["Pitch and loudness", "Pitch depends on how many times per second a wave vibrates, while loudness is measured in decibels on a scale where every 10 more means ten times the sound energy.",
      "Pitch depends on frequency, the number of waves passing each second, measured in hertz. The A above middle C, used to tune orchestras, is 440 hertz. Young people typically hear about 20 to 20,000 hertz, shrinking with age. Elephants use infrasound below our range, while dogs, bats and dolphins hear ultrasound above it. Overtones make instruments sound different on the same note.\n\nLoudness is measured in decibels on a logarithmic scale: every 10 decibels means ten times more sound energy, which we hear as roughly twice as loud. A library is around 30 to 40, conversation around 60 and a loud concert well over 100. Long or repeated exposure above roughly 85 decibels can permanently damage the hair cells in your inner ear. Healthdirect can help with ringing or muffled hearing."],
    ["Echoes and the Doppler effect", "Returning echoes let animals and machines see with sound, and a moving source's pitch rises as it approaches and falls as it leaves.",
      "Shout across a valley and the sound bounces back as an echo. Many bats send out rapid, high-pitched calls and judge the distance, size and texture of insects from the echoes, in total darkness. Dolphins use clicks underwater. This is echolocation. Ships use sonar to map the seafloor, and medical ultrasound builds an image from echoes off organs, which is how many parents first see their baby.\n\nAn ambulance siren drops in pitch as it passes, though the siren has not changed. As it approaches, waves bunch up and arrive more often; as it leaves, they stretch out. This is the Doppler effect, described by Christian Doppler in 1842. It works for all waves: police radar measures car speed, weather radar tracks wind in storms, and astronomers measure how fast galaxies move away."],
    ["Silence and sonic booms", "Sound needs something to travel through, and anything that outruns it creates a sonic boom.",
      "Films are full of roaring explosions in space, but real space is silent. Sound is a vibration passed from particle to particle, and the near vacuum between planets has almost none to carry it. Astronauts on a spacewalk talk by radio, which uses electromagnetic waves that need no medium.\n\nBack on Earth, something interesting happens when an object travels faster than sound. A supersonic jet outruns its own pressure waves, which pile up into a cone-shaped shock wave. When it sweeps over you, you hear a sudden sonic boom. The first officially confirmed supersonic flight was made by American pilot Chuck Yeager in the Bell X-1 rocket plane in 1947. A cracking whip makes a tiny sonic boom too."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What actually travels from a sound source to your ear?", ["A stream of air rushing towards you","A pressure wave passed between molecules","Tiny particles of sound","Light that your ears detect"], 1, "The air itself barely moves; the pressure ripple does the travelling."],
    [1, "Thunder arrives six seconds after the lightning. Roughly how far away is the storm?", ["About 600 metres","About six kilometres","About twenty kilometres","About two kilometres"], 3, "Sound covers about a kilometre every three seconds, so six seconds is roughly two kilometres."],
    [2, "An increase of 10 decibels means about how much more sound energy?", ["Twice as much","Ten per cent more","A hundred times as much","Ten times as much"], 3, "The decibel scale is logarithmic, so small increases in the number are big jumps in energy."],
    [3, "Why does an ambulance siren drop in pitch as it passes you?", ["The driver switches the siren tone as it passes","The sound slows down once it has passed you","Waves bunch up, then stretch out as it leaves","Your ears tire of the sound after a while"], 2, "The motion of the source changes how often waves reach you, which shifts the pitch."],
    [4, "Why would an explosion in space be silent?", ["Space is too cold for any vibration","Light travels too fast for sound to form","Almost no particles exist to carry it","Astronauts' helmets block all noise"], 2, "Sound is a vibration passed between particles, and the near vacuum has almost none."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"You see lightning first for a reason. Count the gap and you can measure the storm."});

WB.L("photosynthesis", "science", "beginner", "Photosynthesis: how plants eat sunlight",
  "A towering gum tree is built mostly from thin air, and the process that does it also made the oxygen you are breathing.",
  [
    ["Built from thin air", "A tree's mass comes mostly from carbon dioxide in the air, not from the soil.",
      "In the 1600s, the Flemish scientist Jan Baptist van Helmont planted a small willow in carefully weighed, dried soil and gave it nothing but water for five years. The willow gained a great deal of weight, yet the soil had barely changed. He concluded the tree was made from water. He was partly right, but he missed the most surprising ingredient.\n\nMost of a plant's dry mass is carbon, and that carbon comes from carbon dioxide drawn out of the air. A huge gum tree is, in a very real sense, built from thin air, stitched together with water and powered by sunlight. The process that does this is called photosynthesis."],
    ["The basic recipe", "Plants use light energy to turn carbon dioxide and water into sugar, and the oxygen they release comes from the water, not the carbon dioxide.",
      "A plant draws water up from its roots and takes in carbon dioxide through tiny leaf pores called stomata. Using the energy in sunlight, it rearranges these into glucose, a sugar, and releases oxygen. In shorthand, six carbon dioxide plus six water become one glucose and six oxygen. Glucose is the plant's food and building material: burned for energy, stored as starch, or linked into cellulose for cell walls and wood. Almost every food you eat traces back to this reaction.\n\nIt seems natural that the oxygen comes from the carbon dioxide. But 1940s experiments with a traceable heavy oxygen showed it comes from water, which plants rip apart using light. Plants do this at ordinary temperatures, and researchers are still trying to copy it to make clean fuels such as hydrogen."],
    ["Why leaves are green", "Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light and reflects green, which is the colour we see.",
      "The green of leaves comes from chlorophyll, a pigment packed inside structures called chloroplasts. Chlorophyll is very good at absorbing red and blue light, which powers photosynthesis. It is poor at absorbing green, so much of that light bounces back towards our eyes.\n\nLeaves also contain yellow and orange pigments called carotenoids. In many deciduous trees, autumn reveals them. As days shorten, the tree stops making chlorophyll and breaks down what it has, and the hidden yellows and oranges show through. Red autumn colours are different: they often come from new pigments made in the leaf at that time of year.\n\nMost Australian native trees, including eucalypts, are evergreen, so our famous autumn displays usually come from introduced species."],
    ["Two stages, many tricks", "Light reactions capture energy and a second stage builds sugar, and some plants adapt the process to cope with heat and drought.",
      "Photosynthesis has two linked stages in the chloroplast. First, chlorophyll absorbs light, splits water, releases oxygen and charges two energy-carrying molecules, ATP and NADPH, like freshly charged batteries. Second, in the Calvin cycle, those batteries power the building of sugar. An enzyme called RuBisCO grabs carbon dioxide from the air and fixes it into an organic molecule. RuBisCO is slow and sometimes grabs oxygen by mistake, so plants make vast amounts of it. It is probably the most abundant protein on Earth.\n\nOpening stomata lets water escape, a problem in hot, dry places. Sugarcane, maize and Australia's spinifex grasses use C4 photosynthesis, concentrating carbon dioxide around RuBisCO. Cacti, pineapples and many succulents use CAM, opening their stomata only at night, storing carbon dioxide as an acid and using it by day with pores shut."],
    ["A planet remade", "Microbes that learned to photosynthesise filled the air with oxygen and transformed Earth.",
      "Photosynthesis is far older than plants. Microbes developed it first, and one group, the cyanobacteria, worked out how to split water and release oxygen. Over hundreds of millions of years that oxygen built up, and around 2.4 billion years ago the atmosphere changed dramatically in the Great Oxidation Event.\n\nSome early oxygen reacted with iron dissolved in the oceans, helping to form banded iron formations. Western Australia's Pilbara holds spectacular examples, and they supply much of the iron ore the country exports.\n\nToday, tiny ocean plankton are thought to produce around half of the world's oxygen, with land plants providing much of the rest. Even the coal, oil and gas we burn are stored sunlight, captured by photosynthesis millions of years ago."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where does most of a tree's dry mass come from?", ["The soil","Minerals in rainwater","Carbon dioxide from the air","Sunlight turning into matter"], 2, "Plants build their bodies mainly from carbon taken from carbon dioxide in the air."],
    [1, "Where does the oxygen released by plants come from?", ["Carbon dioxide","Water molecules split using light energy","Glucose","Soil minerals"], 1, "Tracer experiments showed plants split water, and that is the source of the oxygen."],
    [2, "Why do most leaves look green?", ["Chlorophyll absorbs green light most strongly","Carotenoids are the main green pigment in leaves","Chlorophyll reflects green and absorbs red and blue","Leaves hold green sugar crystals in their cells"], 2, "Leaves look green because chlorophyll reflects the green light it does not absorb."],
    [3, "What is special about CAM plants such as cacti?", ["They grow without needing any light","They take in oxygen, not carbon dioxide","They can only live fully underwater","They open their pores mostly at night"], 3, "Opening stomata when it is cooler loses less water in dry places."],
    [4, "What was the Great Oxidation Event?", ["A giant asteroid strike that reshaped the oceans","A burst of volcanic activity that cooled Earth","Oxygen building up in the air 2.4 billion years ago","The first fires after land plants appeared"], 2, "Photosynthesising microbes released the oxygen that changed Earth's atmosphere and oceans."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A giant gum tree is mostly made of something you cannot see. What is it?"});

WB.L("cells", "science", "beginner", "The cell: life's building block",
  "You are a walking city of tens of trillions of cells, each one a busy factory with its own power stations, post office and instruction library.",
  [
    ["Seeing the first cells", "Cells were first seen in the 1600s, and cell theory says all living things are made of cells and every cell comes from an existing cell.",
      "In 1665 Robert Hooke looked at a thin slice of cork through his microscope and saw rows of tiny empty boxes. They reminded him of the small rooms monks lived in, so he called them cells. He was seeing the walls of dead plant cells. Soon after, the Dutch draper Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, who ground his own lenses, found pond water and tooth scrapings swarming with tiny living creatures: bacteria and other single-celled organisms nobody had seen before.\n\nIn the late 1830s Schleiden and Schwann argued that plants and animals are built from cells. In the 1850s Rudolf Virchow popularised the key addition: every cell comes from another cell. This overturned the belief that life springs from mud or rotting meat, and it let medicine understand disease as something going wrong in cells."],
    ["Trillions and a smart border", "An adult has roughly 30 trillion cells, each wrapped in a thin membrane that controls what gets in and out.",
      "Recent estimates put an adult human at around 30 trillion cells, thousands of times more than the number of people on Earth. Most, by count, are red blood cells: tiny discs with no nucleus, built to carry oxygen. Some nerve fibres run a metre from spine to foot. You also carry a similar number of bacteria, mostly in your gut.\n\nEvery cell is wrapped in a membrane only a few billionths of a metre thick. It is a double layer of fatty molecules called phospholipids, with water-loving heads outside and water-avoiding tails inside. But a cell needs food in and waste out, so the membrane is studded with proteins that act as gates, pumps and messengers. Some let only certain substances through. Others receive signals from hormones or other cells."],
    ["Inside the factory", "Cells contain specialised parts that store instructions, build proteins and supply energy.",
      "A typical animal or plant cell is crowded with specialised parts called organelles. The nucleus is the control centre, holding the DNA with instructions for building proteins. Those instructions are copied and sent out, where ribosomes, tiny molecular machines, read them and assemble proteins one building block at a time.\n\nA folded network called the endoplasmic reticulum helps make and transport many proteins, and the Golgi apparatus packages and labels them for delivery, a bit like a post office. Mitochondria release energy from food, producing ATP, a molecule that powers almost everything the cell does.\n\nPlant cells also have chloroplasts for photosynthesis and a stiff wall for support. Bacteria are simpler cells with no nucleus; their DNA simply floats inside."],
    ["Bacteria within us", "Mitochondria are descended from free-living bacteria that were swallowed by another cell long ago.",
      "One of biology's most surprising ideas is that parts of your cells were once separate living things. The theory, championed by Lynn Margulis from the late 1960s, says mitochondria began as free-living bacteria. More than a billion years ago, one cell engulfed another but did not digest it. The two formed a partnership: the guest supplied energy and the host supplied shelter and food. Over time the guest became a permanent part of the cell.\n\nThe evidence is strong. Mitochondria have their own small loop of DNA, similar to bacterial DNA, and they divide on their own, much as bacteria do. Chloroplasts in plants appear to have the same kind of origin, from captured cyanobacteria. You inherit your mitochondria from your mother."],
    ["Dividing, renewing, going wrong", "Cells constantly divide to grow and repair the body, and cancer happens when mutations break the controls on that division.",
      "You began as a single fertilised egg, and every one of your trillions of cells came from that cell dividing again and again. Before dividing, a cell copies its DNA, then splits so each new cell gets a full set. This continues throughout life. The lining of your gut is renewed every few days, and red blood cells last about four months. Most nerve cells in your brain, however, are thought to last most of your life.\n\nDivision is tightly controlled by signals telling a cell when to grow, stop or die. Cancer is what happens when mutations break those controls, so cells keep dividing when they should not. Understanding the cell is why modern cancer treatments can target specific faults."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which idea is part of cell theory?", ["Life can arise from rotting meat","Only animals are made of cells","Cells are made of tiny units of life","Every cell comes from another cell"], 3, "Cells arise only from existing cells, which overturned the idea of spontaneous generation."],
    [1, "What does the cell membrane do?", ["It controls what goes in and out","It stores the cell's DNA safely","It seals the cell off completely","It makes energy for the whole cell"], 0, "The membrane is a flexible border that lets in food, removes waste and receives signals."],
    [2, "What do ribosomes do?", ["Store DNA inside the nucleus","Release energy from food","Build proteins from instructions","Form the stiff plant cell wall"], 2, "Ribosomes read copied instructions and assemble proteins one building block at a time."],
    [3, "What is a key piece of evidence that mitochondria were once bacteria?", ["They are found only in plants","They have their own DNA, like bacteria","They are green","They can live outside the body"], 1, "Having their own loop of DNA and dividing independently points to a bacterial ancestry."],
    [4, "What happens in cancer?", ["Cells stop copying their DNA completely","Cells die faster than the body can replace them","Nerve cells begin dividing at a normal rate","Mutations break the controls on cell division"], 3, "Cancer is a failure of the signals that tell cells when to grow, stop or die."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"You carry about as many bacteria as human cells, and your mitochondria began as one."});

WB.L("radioactivity", "science", "intermediate", "Radioactivity: the restless atom",
  "Some atoms are quietly falling apart all around you, and learning to understand them gave us carbon dating, cancer treatment and nuclear power.",
  [
    ["Discovered by accident", "Radioactivity was discovered in 1896 when uranium fogged photographic plates, and Marie Curie went on to name it and find two new elements.",
      "In 1896 Henri Becquerel was testing whether uranium salts, after soaking up sunlight, gave off X-rays. Cloudy Paris weather interrupted him, so he put the salts and wrapped photographic plates in a drawer. When he developed the plates anyway, they showed clear images. The uranium was giving off invisible, penetrating radiation all on its own.\n\nMarie Curie found the rays' strength depended on the amount of uranium, not its chemical form, so it came from inside the atom. She coined the word radioactivity. With her husband Pierre she discovered polonium, named for her homeland, and radium in 1898. She won the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics and the 1911 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, the first person to win two. She died in 1934 of an illness linked to radiation exposure; her notebooks are still radioactive."],
    ["Alpha, beta and gamma", "Unstable atoms release three main kinds of radiation, each with very different penetrating power.",
      "An atom is radioactive when its nucleus has an unstable mix of protons and neutrons. To settle down, it throws off energy or particles, called decay. In the early 1900s Ernest Rutherford and others identified three main types.\n\nAlpha particles are clumps of two protons and two neutrons. They are heavy and slow, and a sheet of paper or the outer layer of your skin stops them, though they are harmful if breathed in or swallowed. Beta particles are fast electrons, stopped by a thin sheet of aluminium. Gamma rays are high-energy electromagnetic waves, like extremely powerful X-rays, and need thick lead or concrete to block them.\n\nAlpha or beta decay turns an atom into a different element."],
    ["Predictable randomness", "No one can predict when a single atom will decay, but a large group decays at a perfectly regular rate called its half-life.",
      "Radioactive decay is truly random. There is no way to predict when any particular unstable atom will break down. Yet in a large collection of atoms, the pattern is beautifully regular. The half-life is the time for half the atoms in a sample to decay. After one half-life, half remain; after two, a quarter; after three, an eighth.\n\nHalf-lives range from tiny fractions of a second to billions of years. Uranium-238 has a half-life of about 4.5 billion years, roughly the age of the Earth. Carbon-14, constantly made in the atmosphere and absorbed by living things, has a half-life of about 5,730 years. Measuring how much is left in old bones, wood or charcoal lets scientists date them back roughly 50,000 years."],
    ["Splitting the atom", "Splitting heavy atoms releases enormous energy, which can power cities or, in weapons, destroy them.",
      "In late 1938, Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann found that bombarding uranium with neutrons produced barium, a much lighter element. Their former colleague Lise Meitner and her nephew Otto Frisch worked out why: the nucleus had split in two. They called it fission.\n\nThe pieces weigh slightly less than the original atom, and the missing mass becomes a huge amount of energy, as Einstein's E equals mc squared predicts. Each split also frees more neutrons, which can split more atoms in a chain reaction. Controlled, the heat boils water to drive turbines in a nuclear power station. Uncontrolled, it is the basis of the atomic bomb.\n\nAustralia holds some of the world's largest known uranium resources, and whether it should have nuclear power remains a lively debate."],
    ["Radiation in daily life", "Everyone receives low levels of natural background radiation, and carefully controlled radiation is one of medicine's most valuable tools.",
      "Everyone receives low-level natural background radiation from rocks and soil containing uranium, thorium and potassium, from radon gas seeping out of the ground, and from cosmic rays, which is why long-haul flights add a little extra. Even bananas and your own body contain slightly radioactive potassium. The doses are tiny. In Australia, ARPANSA oversees radiation safety and publishes clear information.\n\nThe same property that makes radiation dangerous also makes it useful. Aimed radiotherapy beams destroy cancer cells while sparing as much healthy tissue as possible. Short-lived radioactive tracers reveal how the heart, bones or thyroid work. Technetium-99m has a half-life of about six hours and is made from materials produced at the OPAL reactor at Lucas Heights in Sydney. Talk to your doctor about any specific scan or treatment."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Becquerel's developed photographic plates show?", ["Photographic plates glow in the dark","Uranium emits rays even without sunlight","Uranium only emits rays after soaking up sun","Cloudy weather itself produces X-rays"], 1, "The plates darkened even in a drawer, so the radiation came from the uranium itself."],
    [1, "Which type of radiation can be stopped by a sheet of paper?", ["Gamma rays","Beta particles","Alpha particles","X-rays"], 2, "Alpha particles are heavy and slow, though they are harmful if breathed in or swallowed."],
    [2, "A sample has a half-life of 10 years. How much is left after 20 years?", ["None","Half","An eighth","A quarter"], 3, "Each half-life halves what remains, so two half-lives leave a quarter."],
    [3, "Where does the energy released in nuclear fission come from?", ["Sunlight absorbed by the uranium","Chemical burning of the uranium","Some missing mass turned into energy","Static electricity inside the reactor"], 2, "The pieces weigh slightly less than the original nucleus, and the difference becomes energy."],
    [4, "Why are you a little radioactive yourself?", ["You absorb radiation from your phone","Your body holds trace radioactive potassium","Radon is made inside your own cells","Cosmic rays have rewritten your DNA"], 1, "Natural background radiation is everywhere, including in food and in your own body, at very low doses."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A cloudy day in Paris in 1896 revealed a hidden property of matter. What was it?"});

WB.L("heat-temperature", "science", "intermediate", "Heat and temperature: what 'hot' really means",
  "Why does a tiled floor feel colder than carpet in the same room? The answer reveals what heat and temperature really are.",
  [
    ["The cold tile mystery", "Tiles feel colder than carpet at the same temperature because they pull heat away from your skin faster, and heat is energy flowing, not the same thing as temperature.",
      "Step barefoot from carpet onto bathroom tiles on a winter morning and the tiles feel icy, though a thermometer would show both at the same temperature. Your skin does not sense temperature directly. It senses how quickly heat flows out of it. Tiles conduct heat well, so they pull warmth from your feet fast. Carpet traps air, a poor conductor, so it feels warmer. The same trick makes metal playground equipment burn in summer while a wooden bench feels merely warm.\n\nEverything is made of moving atoms and molecules. Temperature measures how vigorously they move, on average. Heat is energy flowing from a hotter object to a cooler one. A bathtub of warm water holds far more thermal energy than a cup of boiling water, because it has vastly more molecules."],
    ["Absolute zero and scales", "Nothing can be colder than absolute zero, minus 273.15 degrees Celsius, where particle motion is at its minimum.",
      "If temperature is jiggling, there must be a point where the jiggling is as small as it can be: absolute zero, minus 273.15 degrees Celsius. The Kelvin scale, named after Belfast-born Lord Kelvin, starts there, with steps the same size as Celsius degrees, so water freezes at about 273 kelvin. Atoms have been cooled to within billionths of a degree of it, where some metals become superconductors and some liquids flow without friction.\n\nIn the early 1700s Daniel Fahrenheit built accurate mercury thermometers, with water freezing at 32 degrees and boiling at 212. Anders Celsius proposed 100 degrees between the two; his original ran upside down, until it was flipped. Australia switched from Fahrenheit to Celsius in the early 1970s, which is why older Australians still talk of a 'century' for a 100 degree day."],
    ["Three ways heat moves", "Heat travels by conduction through materials, by convection in moving fluids and by radiation through space.",
      "Conduction is heat passing directly through a material, particle bumping particle, like a metal spoon warming in hot soup. Convection happens in liquids and gases, which can flow. Warm fluid is less dense and rises, cooler fluid sinks, and the circulation carries heat around. That is how a heater warms a room.\n\nRadiation needs no material at all. Every warm object gives off infrared light, and that is how the Sun's warmth crosses 150 million kilometres of empty space to reach your face.\n\nA vacuum flask blocks all three: the vacuum between its walls stops conduction and convection, and shiny surfaces reflect radiation back where it came from."],
    ["Why water heats slowly", "Water needs a lot of energy to warm up, which moderates coastal weather, and a lot to evaporate, which makes sweat an effective coolant.",
      "Put a metal saucepan and the water inside it on the stove, and the pan gets hot long before the water does. Water has an unusually high heat capacity: it takes a lot of energy to raise its temperature, and it releases that energy slowly as it cools.\n\nThis shapes weather. Oceans warm and cool far more slowly than land, so coastal cities such as Perth and Sydney have milder swings in temperature than inland towns such as Alice Springs. On a hot afternoon, land heats faster than the sea, warm air rises over the land, and cooler air flows in from the ocean as a sea breeze.\n\nWater also takes a lot of energy to evaporate, which is why evaporating sweat carries so much heat away from your skin."],
    ["How a fridge moves heat", "A fridge does not make cold: it pumps heat from inside to outside using a fluid that evaporates and condenses.",
      "Heat naturally flows from hot to cold, never the other way on its own. A fridge pushes it uphill, from the cold inside to the warmer kitchen, and pays for this with electricity.\n\nThe trick is a refrigerant circulating in a closed loop of pipes. Inside the fridge, it evaporates at low pressure, and evaporating soaks up heat, just as sweat cools your skin. A compressor then squeezes the vapour, making it hot, and it releases that heat through coils at the back or sides, which is why they feel warm. The fluid condenses back to a liquid and the cycle repeats.\n\nReverse-cycle air conditioners and heat pumps work the same way, often moving several units of heat per unit of electricity."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do tiles feel colder than carpet when both are at the same temperature?", ["Tiles are actually colder than carpet","Carpet produces a little heat itself","Tiles soak up moisture from your feet","Tiles draw heat from your skin faster"], 3, "Your skin senses the rate heat leaves it, not the temperature itself."],
    [1, "What is absolute zero?", ["Minus 273.15 degrees Celsius, the coldest possible","Zero degrees Celsius, where water freezes","Minus 100 degrees Celsius, where all motion stops","The lowest temperature ever reached in a lab"], 0, "At absolute zero particle motion is at its minimum, and nothing can be colder."],
    [2, "How does the Sun's heat reach Earth across empty space?", ["Conduction","Convection","Radiation","Evaporation"], 2, "Radiation needs no material, so infrared light can cross a vacuum."],
    [3, "Why do coastal cities usually have milder temperature swings than inland towns?", ["The sea is always colder than the land","Water warms and cools more slowly than land","Coastal air holds much less oxygen","Sea breezes only happen during the night"], 1, "Water's high heat capacity soaks up and releases heat slowly, which steadies nearby weather."],
    [4, "What does a refrigerator actually do?", ["Creates cold and pumps it in","Removes air from inside","Pumps heat from inside to outside","Freezes the refrigerant permanently"], 2, "A fridge moves existing heat uphill using a fluid that evaporates and condenses."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A tiled floor and a carpet sit in the same room. Why does one feel colder?"});

WB.L("enzymes", "science", "advanced", "Enzymes: life's tiny machines",
  "Reactions that would take millions of years on their own happen in your cells in a split second, thanks to molecules called enzymes.",
  [
    ["The problem of slow chemistry", "Most of the reactions life depends on are far too slow without help, and enzymes are proteins that speed up specific reactions without being used up.",
      "Your cells break down sugars, copy DNA, build proteins and clear waste every second. Left alone at body temperature, most of these reactions would crawl along at a hopeless pace, some taking millions of years. A lab chemist might use strong acids, high pressure or fierce heat, but none are options inside a cell. Life solved the problem with enzymes: large molecules, nearly all proteins, that make specific reactions happen enormously faster without being used up. Such molecules are called catalysts.\n\nPeople used enzymes for thousands of years in brewing, baking and cheesemaking. In 1897 Eduard Buchner showed that juice from ground-up yeast, with no living cells, could still ferment sugar. In 1926 James Sumner crystallised the enzyme urease and showed it was a protein."],
    ["Lowering the hill", "Enzymes lower the energy hurdle a reaction must clear, so many more molecules make it over.",
      "Picture a reaction as a ball that must be pushed over a hill before it can roll down the other side. That hill is the activation energy. Even if the products are more stable than the starting materials, few molecules have enough energy to get over the top, so the reaction is slow.\n\nAn enzyme provides an easier route, effectively lowering the hill. It can hold reacting molecules close together in exactly the right orientation, stretch or strain chemical bonds so they break more easily, and position charged parts of itself to stabilise the awkward arrangement at the top of the hill.\n\nAn enzyme does not change where the reaction ends up, only how quickly it gets there, and it emerges unchanged, ready to work again."],
    ["Shape is everything", "An enzyme works because of its precisely folded shape, so heat or acidity that unravels the fold stops it working.",
      "Enzymes are choosy. Most work on one molecule, or a small family, called substrates. An enzyme is a chain of amino acids folded into a precise shape, with a pocket called the active site. In 1894 Emil Fischer likened the fit to a key in a lock. In 1958 Daniel Koshland refined this with induced fit: the active site is flexible and moulds around the substrate, like a glove tightening around a hand.\n\nDisturb the fold and the enzyme stops working. Warming speeds an enzyme up at first, but beyond a point heat shakes the fold apart, called denaturation. You see it in a cooked egg: the white turns solid as its proteins unravel, for good. Most human enzymes work best near body temperature. Pepsin thrives in strong stomach acid; small-intestine enzymes prefer milder conditions."],
    ["Switches and medicines", "Cells control their enzymes with feedback and inhibitors, and many medicines work by blocking enzymes.",
      "A cell cannot leave all its enzymes running at full speed, or it would waste resources and overproduce some products. So enzymes are regulated. Often the end product of a chain of reactions binds to an enzyme near the start and slows it down, a neat feedback that keeps supplies in balance. Other molecules act as inhibitors, either jamming the active site or binding elsewhere and changing the enzyme's shape.\n\nMedicine makes heavy use of this. Aspirin works partly by blocking enzymes involved in making substances that cause pain and inflammation. Penicillin blocks enzymes bacteria need to build their cell walls. Many other drugs target specific enzymes too. This is general information only, so ask a doctor or pharmacist about any particular medicine."],
    ["Enzymes in daily life", "Enzymes quietly work in washing powders, food production and research, and scientists now design new ones.",
      "Biological laundry powders contain enzymes that break down protein, fat and starch stains, so they work well in cooler water. Cheesemakers have long used rennet, which contains an enzyme that curdles milk. Enzymes help clarify fruit juice, make sweet syrups from starch and tenderise meat. Papaya and pineapple contain such enzymes, which is why fresh pineapple can stop jelly setting.\n\nIn research, an enzyme from a hot spring bacterium, able to survive repeated heating, made possible the PCR technique used to copy DNA. Scientists are now improving enzymes and designing new ones. Frances Arnold shared the 2018 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for using directed evolution to breed better enzymes in the lab."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does a catalyst such as an enzyme do?", ["Stores the energy a reaction releases","Becomes one of the reaction's end products","Speeds up a reaction without being used up","Stops a reaction before it finishes"], 2, "Enzymes make specific reactions happen far faster and are left unchanged to work again."],
    [1, "How does an enzyme speed up a reaction?", ["By adding heat to the cell","By lowering the activation energy","By changing the final products","By being consumed in the reaction"], 1, "A lower energy hill lets many more molecules react, but the end point does not change."],
    [2, "What happens when an enzyme is denatured?", ["It becomes far more active than before","It turns into a different kind of enzyme","Its shape unravels and it stops working","It speeds up and keeps going permanently"], 2, "An enzyme's power depends on its folded shape, and too much heat destroys it, as with a cooked egg."],
    [3, "How does penicillin work?", ["It blocks enzymes bacteria use for cell walls","It boosts the enzymes in human immune cells","It denatures every protein in the bacteria","It supplies extra energy to the immune system"], 0, "Many medicines work by inhibiting a specific enzyme."],
    [4, "Why can fresh pineapple stop jelly from setting?", ["It adds too much sugar to the mixture","It is too acidic for water to freeze","It cools the water down too quickly","It contains an enzyme that digests protein"], 3, "Enzymes in fruit such as pineapple and papaya break down the proteins that make jelly set."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Reactions that should take millions of years happen in your cells in a blink. How?"});

WB.L("soil", "science", "intermediate", "The secret life of soil",
  "The ground beneath your feet is not dirt: it is a living, breathing system that took thousands of years to build and feeds almost everything on land.",
  [
    ["A crowded handful", "Healthy soil teems with life, from bacteria and fungi to worms and insects.",
      "A handful of healthy soil is one of the most crowded habitats on Earth. Soil scientists often note that a single teaspoon can hold more microorganisms than there are people on the planet. Bacteria, fungi, single-celled protists and microscopic nematode worms share the space with mites, springtails, beetle larvae, ants and earthworms. Most of these organisms have never been studied, and many have not been named.\n\nThe earthy smell after rain comes partly from geosmin, a compound made by certain soil bacteria. People are remarkably sensitive to it. Far from lifeless dirt, soil is a busy underground city where life is recycled day and night."],
    ["Slow to build", "Soil takes centuries to form from rock, so soil lost to erosion is effectively gone for good.",
      "Soil begins as rock. Heat and cold, freezing water, plant roots and rainwater slowly break it apart. Lichens and mosses colonise the surface and add organic material as they die. Microbes and small animals mix it with mineral grains, and layers form: dark, humus-rich soil near the top, paler mineral layers below, then the parent rock.\n\nThis is extremely slow. A few centimetres of new soil can take hundreds of years or more, so soil lost to erosion is effectively lost for good. In the 1930s, drought and poor farming on the American Great Plains caused the Dust Bowl, when dust storms stripped away topsoil and forced many families off their farms. Soil is also estimated to hold more carbon than the atmosphere and all living plants combined."],
    ["Sand, silt and clay", "The mix of sand, silt and clay controls how soil holds water, air and nutrients.",
      "The mineral part of soil comes in three particle sizes. Sand is the largest and feels gritty. Silt is finer and smooth, a bit like flour. Clay is tiny and sticky when wet. Their balance gives a soil its texture, and texture matters enormously.\n\nSandy soil drains fast and warms quickly but holds little water or nutrients. Clay holds plenty of both but can become waterlogged, and sets hard when dry. Gardeners prize loam, a mix of all three, which holds moisture while still letting air reach the roots. A simple test: roll a moist pinch between your fingers. Clay forms a ribbon, and sand falls apart."],
    ["Fungi and worms at work", "Fungi trade water and nutrients for plant sugars, while worms and decomposers recycle dead material.",
      "Most land plants partner with fungi in relationships called mycorrhizae. The plant supplies sugars from photosynthesis. In return, fungal threads called hyphae spread far beyond the roots and bring back water and nutrients, especially phosphorus. The partnership is ancient and may have helped the first plants colonise land. Claims that forests share food and warnings through a 'wood wide web' are debated, so treat them with caution.\n\nEarthworms swallow soil and decaying matter, leave nutrient-rich castings, and open tunnels for air and water. Darwin devoted his last book, in 1881, to them. Bacteria and fungi break dead material into substances plants can absorb again. Victoria's giant Gippsland earthworm can reach about a metre long."],
    ["Australia's ancient soils", "Many Australian soils are old and low in nutrients, and native plants have adapted in clever ways.",
      "Australia's soils are often very old and very poor. Much of the land has been stable for a long time, without recent glaciers grinding up fresh rock or widespread volcanoes spreading mineral-rich ash. Over millions of years, rain has leached out many nutrients, and phosphorus is often scarce.\n\nNative plants have adapted. Banksias, grevilleas and their relatives grow dense mats of fine cluster roots that release chemicals to free up tightly bound phosphorus. Some natives are even harmed by the phosphorus in ordinary garden fertiliser. Farmers have long added fertilisers such as superphosphate, and scientists study how to farm these soils without degrading them or causing salinity. Serious erosion and dust storms have happened here too. The community Landcare movement began in Victoria in the late 1980s and now has thousands of local groups repairing land."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does the ground smell earthy after rain?", ["Rain releases chlorophyll from the roots of grass","Soil bacteria make geosmin, which people detect easily","Methane bubbles up from wet soil","Humus dissolves into the rainwater"], 1, "Geosmin, made by certain soil bacteria, is a key part of that earthy smell."],
    [1, "Why is soil lost to erosion effectively lost for good?", ["New soil can take hundreds of years to form","New soil forms within a single growing season","Rock can no longer break down into soil","Farmers cannot grow crops in new soil"], 0, "Soil forms extremely slowly, so on a human timescale eroded soil does not come back."],
    [2, "Why do gardeners prize loam?", ["It drains water faster than any other soil","It is made entirely of fine clay particles","It sets hard so roots stay anchored","It holds moisture while still letting air reach the roots"], 3, "Loam mixes sand, silt and clay, balancing water retention with airflow."],
    [3, "What is the trade in a mycorrhizal partnership?", ["The plant supplies water and the fungus supplies sugars","The plant supplies seeds and the fungus supplies oxygen","The plant supplies sugars and the fungus supplies water and nutrients","The plant supplies nitrogen and the fungus supplies sunlight"], 2, "Plants pay in photosynthesis sugars, and fungi return water and nutrients such as phosphorus."],
    [4, "How have banksias adapted to phosphorus-poor soil?", ["They grow only in swamps","They grow cluster roots that free up bound phosphorus","They absorb phosphorus from the air","They trap insects to digest for phosphorus"], 1, "Cluster roots release chemicals that unlock scarce phosphorus from the soil."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Soil scientists say one teaspoon can hold more life than there are people."});

